# Hostile audit — mixed-phase matching-splice train deficit

**Date:** 2026-09-15\
**Audited source:** `research/target_c_mixed_flower_matching_splice_deficit_2026-09-15.md`\
**Verdict:** **PASS at proof-only asymptotic scope.**\
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The theorem claims that a linear family of local through-pair flower connectors, even
with unrelated limiting phases, contains a vertex-disjoint subfamily whose two-piece
supertrains have a simple intersection graph.  The resulting global train-crossing
deficit is at least `L^2/18-o(D^2)` for `L` local through pairs.

The audit targets the five vulnerable steps: matching size, supertrain simplicity,
connector interference, the exact `60`-degree crossing threshold, and the final edge
count.

## 1. The splice graph really has maximum degree two

One selected flower contributes one edge joining the two distinct complete trains used
by its local through pair.  A train cannot supply both endpoints to the same bounded
face, so there is no loop.  Every complete train has exactly two terminal occurrences,
so it can appear in at most two selected flower edges.  Parallel splice edges between
the same two train vertices do not break the degree bound.

Thus the splice multigraph is loopless with maximum degree at most two.

A greedy matching step selects one edge and removes at most three edges: the selected
edge plus at most one other at each endpoint.  Hence at least `ceil(L/3)` edges can be
selected.  This bound remains true for triangles, odd cycles, and parallel-edge
components, so no hidden bipartiteness or path assumption is present.

## 2. A matched two-piece supertrain is simple

The two constituent train projective directions differ by `epsilon_D->0`.  For large
`D` this is below `pi/3`, while a strict-rhombus crossing requires projective
separation strictly greater than `pi/3`.  The two constituent train paths therefore do
not cross one another.

Each train midpoint path is simple.  The connector lies in the interior of the selected
bounded exceptional face and meets the contact complex only at its two terminal
midpoints.  The concatenation is therefore simple.

Matching is essential here: no train belongs to two selected splices, so a supertrain
contains exactly two train pieces and cannot accumulate phase drift along a long
splice chain.

## 3. Connectors cannot create hidden intersections

Different selected connectors lie in interiors of distinct bounded faces, whose
interiors are disjoint.  A stopped train midpoint path runs through strict rhombi and
terminates at an exceptional-face boundary; it does not enter that face interior.
Thus a connector cannot intersect an unrelated train piece or another selected
connector in its interior.

Every intersection of two supertrains is consequently one original strict-rhombus
intersection of constituent stopped trains.

## 4. Pairwise common projection works uniformly

Take two matched supertrains with representative projective directions `alpha,beta`
and choose representatives with separation

\[
0\le d=\beta-\alpha\le\pi/2.
\]

Every segment of the first supertrain lies within `pi/6+o(1)` of `Jalpha`, and every
segment of the second within the same cone around `Jbeta`.  The angular bisector of
these normals is at distance at most

\[
\pi/4+\pi/6+o(1)=5\pi/12+o(1)<\pi/2
\]

from every segment.  Hence whenever crossings are directionally possible, both
supertrains are strictly monotone graphs over one pair-specific coordinate.

If `d<pi/3-O(epsilon_D)`, no constituent direction pair can satisfy the strict
rhombus threshold and there is no crossing.  No uniform gap from `pi/3` is needed.

## 5. The exact `pi/3` threshold does not allow opposite crossing signs

This is the main hostile check.

For one actual rhombus crossing choose oriented projective representatives `u,v` so
`v` lies counterclockwise from `u` and their projective separation is in
`(pi/3,pi/2]`.  Canonical positive-normal orientation of the `u` train makes its local
midpoint displacement `+v`.  Canonical positive-normal orientation of the `v` train
makes its local displacement `-u`, since

\[
\det(v,-u)=\det(u,v)>0.
\]

Thus the graph-crossing sign is determined solely by the ordered pair of narrow
projective windows and is the same at every crossing between the two supertrains.
This remains true when their limiting separation is exactly `pi/3`: only constituent
pairs whose finite separation has moved to the crossing side `>pi/3` can intersect,
and all such pairs have the same orientation.

Two monotone graphs cannot cross twice with the same transverse sign.  Therefore any
two matched supertrains meet at most once.

The same argument applies when one of the two supertrains is unspliced.

## 6. No rhombus is lost inside a matched component

The two trains merged by one matching edge have direction mismatch `o(1)<pi/3`, so
they do not cross in a strict rhombus.  Hence no original rhombus becomes an internal
self-intersection after splicing.

Every original strict rhombus maps to an intersection of two distinct supertrains.
Pairwise simplicity prevents two rhombi from mapping to the same unordered
supertrain pair.  Therefore the supertrain intersection graph has exactly `R_D`
edges, not merely at most `R_D`.

## 7. The final combinatorial algebra is correct

With `P` matched splices there are

\[
r=P
\]

two-piece supertrains and

\[
s=T-2P
\]

unspliced original trains.  The induced crossing graph on the `s` unspliced trains is
triangle-free, so it has at most `s^2/4` edges.  Pairwise simplicity gives at most

\[
rs+\binom r2
\]

additional edges incident with a two-piece supertrain.  Therefore

\[
R\le {s^2\over4}+rs+\binom r2
={T^2\over4}-{P^2\over2}-{P\over2}.
\]

The companion finite regression verifies this identity over a large integer box and
checks

\[
P\ge L/3\Longrightarrow P^2/2\ge L^2/18.
\]

The favorable linear `-P/2` term is discarded in the normalized theorem.

## 8. Scope of the flower composition

The three-dominant local theorem supplies through pairs with one uniform mismatch and
connector error scale tending to zero over the selected flower population, by
compactness.  Therefore it satisfies the hypotheses of the matching theorem.

What is **not** proved is a global finite source deformation, a diameter decrease, or
a contradiction from the new deficit alone.  Numerically the existing full-flower
branch already has a larger aggregate Mantel deficit.  The new content is a deficit
certified by actual local through mechanisms and valid across mixed complement phases.
That structure can now be coupled to diameter bracing or closure rank without first
forcing all splices into one dominant orbit.

**Final verdict:** PASS at proof-only asymptotic scope.
